   169	- **Multiple factions attacking one territory** → every attacker takes losses against
   170	  the defender. The largest surviving force takes the territory.
   171	
   172	The 1:1 loss ratio is the primary brake on snowballing: taking ground is expensive, so
   173	a leader cannot cheaply convert an advantage into a runaway.
   174	
   175	Removing dice is deliberate. The variance budget is spent entirely on the prediction
   176	markets. Two stacked random systems would make outcomes feel arbitrary rather than
   177	earned, and would drown out the thing that makes the market layer interesting — that
   178	its uncertainty is reasoned rather than noise.
   179	
   180	The multi-attacker rule is what gives handshake alliances teeth. Two allies can take a
   181	territory neither could take alone, both bleed for it, and only one of them keeps it.
   182	
   183	### Elimination and protection
   184	
   185	A faction with zero territories is eliminated. It cannot deploy, attack, or return to
   186	the map. Instead it gains a **daily protection**: it names one territory anywhere on the
   187	map, and every attack targeting that territory is voided.
   188	
   189	This is a role change, not an exit. The eliminated player becomes a kingmaker that
   190	living factions have to negotiate with, which in practice means more Slack conversation
   191	than they had while playing.
   192	
   193	Four rules govern it:
   194	
   195	**It requires a workout.** The protection only fires if that player has at least one
   196	approved IRL action that day. No photo, no veto. This is what keeps elimination inside
   197	the accountability loop rather than turning it into free power for doing nothing — the
   198	whole reason the mechanic exists.
   199	
   200	**Picks are secret.** Submitted in the normal order window, revealed in the recap. If
   201	picks were public, attackers would simply route around them and the veto would be a
   202	mild inconvenience. Secrecy makes it a real threat that shapes how everyone plans.
   203	
   204	**Blocked attacks are voided, not destroyed.** A cancelled attack leaves its troops in
   205	the origin territory. Combined with secrecy, a blocked attack costs a tempo rather than
   206	an army — the difference between a fun surprise and a season-ending gotcha. Destroying
   207	armies on a hidden condition would feel arbitrary and punishing.
   208	
   209	**Protections toggle by parity.** A territory is protected if an *odd* number of
   210	eliminated factions named it. Two picks cancel out, three protect again:
   211	
   212	```
   213	protected(t) = count(picks on t, from factions with an approved action today) % 2 === 1
   214	```
   215	
   216	Because picks are secret, eliminated players cancel each other blind. Two allies of
   217	opposing living factions can neutralize each other without knowing it, and a living
   218	faction can lobby one eliminated player into cancelling another's shield. This also
   219	self-limits board lockup late in a season, when several eliminated players might
   220	otherwise freeze a territory each.
   221	
   222	Protection is a shield, not a stasis field: the owner can still deploy into a protected
   223	territory and still attack out of it. Only incoming attacks are affected. Any territory
   224	is a legal pick, not just ones the eliminated faction used to own.
   225	
   226	Mechanically this sits at the head of step 6 — resolve parity, then drop every attack
   227	targeting a protected territory, then sum what remains.
   228	
   229	## Economy
   230	
   231	### Baseline income
   232	
   233	`max(3, floor(territories / 3))` plus continent bonuses. Typical mid-game is 4–7 per
   234	day. Everything else is sized against this anchor.
   235	
   236	### IRL actions
   237	
   238	**Maximum 2 approved actions per day, +1 soldier each.**
   239	
   240	Deliberately small. A max-effort player earns roughly 25–40% more than someone who
   241	posts nothing — a real advantage that good play can overcome. Over a 21-day season
   242	that is about 42 extra soldiers: meaningful, never decisive.
   243	
   244	Effort is a participation floor, not the lever that decides the game. Strategy and
   245	wagers decide the game.
   246	
   247	### Timing bonuses
   248	
   249	Two extra soldiers are available each day for *when* an action lands:
   250	
   251	- **Early Bird** — the first player with an approved action that day: +1
   252	- **Under the Wire** — the last approved action before the tick cutoff: +1
   253	
   254	Maximum one timing bonus per player per day, granted on top of the 2-action cap. Peak
   255	daily IRL income is therefore 3, still small against 4–7 baseline territory income, so
   256	the balance sizing above is unchanged.
   257	
   258	These attach to workout photos and **never to order submission**. Order timing must stay
   259	neutral: orders are freely editable until lock, and market prices are frozen at 08:00,
   260	precisely so nothing is gained by submitting late. A bonus for submitting last would pay
   261	the position that already holds the most information — having watched the markets move
   262	and read the day's Slack traffic — and a bonus for submitting first would reward having
   263	a free 8am, which is the schedule-flexibility advantage the action cap exists to
   264	suppress. It would also be trivially gamed by submitting an empty order at 08:01 and
   265	editing it all day.
   266	
   267	Photo timing leaks no strategic information, so there is nothing to exploit, and it
   268	rewards actually exercising in the morning rather than intending to all day.
   269	
   270	If Early Bird is won by the same early riser every day and goes stale, make it a
   271	rotating exclusion — no player may take it twice in a row — rather than removing it.
   272	
   273	### Approval is social, not adversarial
   274	
   275	An action counts when a player posts a photo in `#riskety-rekt` and two *other*
   276	players react with 👍 before the tick cutoff. You cannot approve your own.
   277	
   278	Among friends, everyone will approve everything. Design as if that is true. The real
   279	job of peer approval is making people see each other's workouts, which is the part that
   280	drives the habit. Do not add complexity trying to make this tamper-proof; it cannot be
   281	and does not need to be.
   282	
   283	### Wagers
   284	
   285	Each morning, 3–5 markets closing that day are published as the slate. Players stake
   286	soldiers from their reserve on YES or NO.
   287	
   288	**Payout on a win:** `floor(stake / p * 1.1)`, where `p` is the snapshotted price of
   289	the chosen side. A loss returns nothing.
   290	
   291	Fair odds would make expected value exactly zero, which makes wagering a *variance*
   292	tool rather than an income stream — the right property for a comeback mechanic. The
   293	player who is behind should take underdog bets; the leader should sit out. The 1.1×
   294	multiplier is a small house bonus so that engaging with the slate is mildly correct
   295	every day even for a comfortable leader, since the daily ritual is half the point.
   296	
   297	`p` is clamped to `[0.05, 0.95]` as a safety net against absurd payouts, though slate
   298	filtering should prevent extreme prices from appearing at all.
   299	
   300	**No stake cap.** All-in is allowed and the chaos is intended. This is self-balancing
   301	without a cap: reserves are only *undeployed* soldiers, so building a large stake means
   302	hoarding, and hoarding means leaving the map thin. The all-in is already paid for.
   303	
   304	**No slate on the final day.** Wagers placed on day N pay out at tick N+1, so a day 21
   305	stake could never be spent. Publishing no slate on the last day avoids burning reserves
   306	on nothing.
   307	
   308	### Market selection
   309	
   310	Candidates must close before 21:00 ET the same day, clear a volume floor so they cannot
   311	be cheaply moved, and be priced between 0.10 and 0.90. Pick 3–5.
   312	
   313	The volume floor is set from observed data rather than guessed: during implementation,
   314	sample a week of Kalshi same-day markets and set the floor at the median. Store it as
   315	config so it can be raised without a deploy.
   316	
   317	**Prices are snapshotted once**, when the slate is published at 08:00, and that price
   318	applies to everyone regardless of when they submit. Pricing at submission time would
   319	give whoever submits last strictly better information — they could watch a market move
   320	on breaking news and bet into a stale line.
   321	
   322	**Sources:** Kalshi is primary. Same-day settlement is its core product, so a reliable
   323	supply of markets closing on a given day exists. Manifold is the development and
   324	fallback source; its API is open and unauthenticated.
   325	
   326	Polymarket is explicitly not used. Its UMA optimistic oracle has a proposal-and-
   327	challenge window that can run from hours to days, which is incompatible with a daily
   328	tick.
   329	
   330	## Daily timeline
   331	
   332	| Time | Event |
   333	|---|---|
   334	| 08:00 | Fetch candidates, filter, pick 3–5, snapshot prices, publish slate to web and Slack |
   335	| all day | Players post workout photos to Slack and react; players submit and revise orders on the web app |
   336	| 21:00 | Lock orders. Read cached approvals and yesterday's settlements. Run the pipeline. Save state. Post recap to Slack with a rendered map. |
   337	
   338	The map image is generated server-side by rendering the board to SVG from `GameState`
   339	and rasterizing it to PNG for Slack. The same SVG renderer backs the web app's board
   340	view, so there is one source of truth for how the map looks.
   341	
   342	The recap deserves real effort. It is the daily artifact everyone actually sees — who
   343	attacked whom, whose bet paid off, who posted their workout. In a friend group that
   344	drives return visits more than the web app does.
   345	
   346	## Failure modes
   347	
   348	**The tick must never be blocked by an outside system.**
   349	
   350	- **Slack approvals are cached as they happen.** The bot writes reaction events to the
   351	  DB continuously via the Events API. At 21:00 the tick reads local state and never
   352	  calls the Slack API, so a Slack outage at tick time is harmless.
   353	- **No market slate → the day runs as plain Risk.** If the market source is unreachable
   354	  at 08:00, publish an empty slate, post a note, carry on. A missing market day is a
   355	  disappointment; a stalled season is fatal.
   356	- **Unsettled markets roll forward.** A market not settled at tick time carries to the
   357	  next tick. Still unsettled after 48 hours, the stake is refunded.
   358	
   359	**Invalid orders are dropped, never thrown.** Validate in the web app for good UX, and
   360	again in the engine — but the engine silently discards a malformed order rather than

